<p class="m-note">The community-network chapter · November 2026</p><h1 class="m-title">The same vision.<br />A more direct starting point.</h1>
<p class="m-lede">We want people to have a real say in the software and networks they live with.</p>
<div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none mt-10">
<p>The earlier xNet explored that idea through a composable data layer, collaborative documents, and an Electron workspace. Those experiments taught us a lot. We’re now putting our effort into a desktop app for friends and communities: conversations, public posts, music, and video.</p>
<h2>Start with people connecting.</h2><p>Share an invitation. Have a conversation. Share something you made. Contribute some storage and bandwidth if you choose. These are useful building blocks for a community before we ask anyone to learn a framework.</p>
<h2>Keep the network understandable.</h2><p>The desktop keeps local data and talks to peers. Small, self-hostable hubs provide discovery and a public index. They can relay bounded live traffic, but media and private chat history come from peers. There are real tradeoffs: a file with no online copy is unavailable, and peer-to-peer does not mean invisible or anonymous.</p>
<h2>Keep room for a different future.</h2><p>Sovereignty, consent, and software we can change together are still the point. Moving to Rust and Tauri is a practical choice for this product, not a rejection of the questions behind it. We are not claiming all the old workspace features exist in the new app.</p>
<p>The blog stays intact. Essays from the workspace era link to the archived implementation, so their technical claims have a home. New essays can follow the community network as it grows.</p>
<p><a href="/blog">Read the essays</a> · <a href="https://github.com/crs48/xNet/blob/main/LEGACY.md">Find the earlier code and research</a></p>
